+### [Sphere-Guided Training Limitations](15291-sphere-guided-training-limitations.md)
+Details inherent limitations of sphere-guided training in neural implicit surface reconstruction, specifically regarding exploration capabilities and appearance-geometry coupling. It addresses the problem where early-stage geometric estimates constrain the optimization trajectory, leading to artifacts in uncovered regions or incorrect geometry baking when combined with appearance warping. Unlike standard volume rendering which samples uniformly or via importance sampling based on density, sphere-guided methods rely on a discrete set of primitives that, once converged, may fail to recover thin structures or correct systematic errors.
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: The complexity depends on the number of spheres $|S|$ and the number of merged intervals $K$. Computing intersections is $O(|S|)$, merging is $O(|S| \log |S|)$ due to sorting, and sampling is $O(K)$. The overall complexity is dominated by the intersection and merging steps [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(|S|)$ to store the sphere cloud and $O(K)$ for the intervals [S1].
-- **Strengths**: Improves sample efficiency by filtering out samples outside the surface region, allowing the implicit function to converge to a better optimum [S1]. It trains the guiding reconstruction from scratch without prior initialization [S1].
-- **Limitations**: Spheres may get stuck in local minima or clump together, requiring additional mechanisms like repulsion and resampling [S1].
-- **When to use**: Suitable for neural implicit surface reconstruction methods like IDR, NeRF, and others, where efficient sampling is critical for convergence [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The sampling step involves iterating over all spheres to find intersections ($O(|S|)$) and merging intervals ($O(|S| \log |S|)$ due to sorting). Sample generation is linear in the number of samples. Overall, it is efficient compared to dense volumetric sampling.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the size of the sphere cloud $|S|$ and the number of samples $n$.
+*   **Strengths:** Improves sample efficiency by filtering out samples outside the surface region. Allows the implicit function to converge to a better optimum [S1]. Uses gradient descent to train the sphere cloud from scratch without prior initialization, unlike greedy optimization methods [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Spheres may clump or get stuck in local minima [S1]. May fail to recover thin structures if the sphere cloud is not sufficiently dense or diverse [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When using base neural reconstruction methods like IDR [37], NeRF [20], or others [5, 23, 32, 36] for surface reconstruction, to improve quality [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When the surface geometry is extremely thin or complex, and the sphere approximation cannot capture it accurately.
 
 ## Background
-Sphere-Guided Volume Rendering was introduced in the paper "Sphere-Guided Training of Neural Implicit Surfaces" [S1]. It addresses limitations in existing methods that use greedy optimization (e.g., Neural Sparse Voxel Fields [17]) or sparse point-cloud initialization (e.g., Neural 3D Reconstruction in the Wild [31]) [S1]. The method is designed to work with modern implicit surface modeling systems [5, 23, 32, 36] and has been shown to provide uniform improvements in reconstruction quality across multiple 3D reconstruction benchmarks [S1].
+Sphere-Guided Volume Rendering was introduced in the paper "Sphere-Guided Training of Neural Implicit Surfaces" [S1]. It addresses the limitations of previous methods that used greedy optimization (e.g., Neural Sparse Voxel Fields [17]) or sparse point-cloud initialization (e.g., Neural 3D Reconstruction in the Wild [31]) [S1]. The method uses gradient descent to train the sphere cloud from scratch, allowing it to be trained alongside the implicit surface field [S1]. It is used in conjunction with modern systems for surface reconstruction to observe uniform improvements in quality across multiple 3D reconstruction benchmarks [S1].
 
